  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing CORS Considerations**\n\nAlright, it looks like CORS won\'t be an issue after all! Since Vite and Express are served on the same host and port, requests to `/api/*` will be handled smoothly. I\'m now turning my attention to the package.json scripts, per those instructions.\n\n\n**Updating Package Scripts**\n\nOkay, I\'ve updated the `package.json` scripts as instructed, incorporating `tsx` and `esbuild`. I confirmed the necessary packages, including `express`, `esbuild`, and `tsx`, are installed and their versions are as required. Now I\'m delving into how `sql.js` functions within a Node.js environment, specifically in TypeScript. I\'m exploring the process of loading and saving SQLite databases, including handling existing or new database files.\n\n\n**Defining Database Interactions**\n\nI\'m now focusing on how `sql.js` executes queries, specifically the methods `db.run`, `db.exec`, and helper functions for typed queries. I\'m moving toward creating a clean, type-safe SQLite database manager, and starting to map the necessary tables and their attributes. I\'ll need `transactions` and `categories` tables, and I\'ve started the attribute planning.\n\n\n**Planning Database Tables**\n\nOkay, I\'m fleshing out the database schema. I\'ve defined the attributes for several new tables: `color`, `monthlyBudget`, and `isCustom` for `categories`, along with `prorated_rules`, `savings_goals`, `savings_history`, and `debts`. I\'m focusing on data types like `TEXT`, `REAL`, and `INTEGER`, incorporating constraints like `NOT NULL` and `DEFAULT` to ensure data integrity. I\'m taking special care when I define the foreign keys.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 3
      nanos: 769021266
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
