  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Creating the Database Service**\n\nOkay, I\'m working on the SQLite database service now, targeting either `/src/server/db.ts` or just `/server/db.ts`. I think `server.ts` will need to import the database service, initialize the schema, seed it if it\'s empty, and then mount the CRUD API endpoints using Express, specifically `app.use(express.json())`. Just checking how I will do that now.\n\n\n**Verifying the sql.js Implementation**\n\nI\'ve been examining `sql.js` in Node.js, specifically the initialization and database operations. I have been focusing on how to import, initialize, and handle writing changes. I\'m confirming helper functions for executing queries and running updates are efficient, using `stmt.getAsObject()` for clean results. I am now checking tables and fields for a transaction.\n\n\n**Defining the Database Schema**\n\nI\'m now concretely defining the SQLite schema. I have the `expenses` table outlined with `amount`, `type`, `category`, `date`, `tags`, `notes`, `paymentMethod`, `isRecurring`, `recurringFrequency`, `receiptUrl`, and `created_at` fields. I have also moved on to defining tables for `categories`, `prorated_rules`, `savings_goals`, `savings_history`, and `debts`.\n\n\n**Expanding the Schema Definition**\n\nI\'ve added the `debts`, `debt_payments`, `recurring_items`, and `user_settings` tables to the SQLite schema definition. These tables include details for managing debt, recording payments, tracking recurring expenses, and storing user preferences. I will need to consider the relationships between these new tables, especially regarding foreign keys and how they interact with other tables in the database.\n\n\n"
